oping something  miraculous will happen but really when  you're making decisions for your  business thinking long term and think  about what is actually going to make a  difference 10 years from now and what's  actually going to make a difference even  two or three years from now but I think  most people get stuck in what's going to  make a difference  this week or this quarter or even this  year and and you know when you think  longer-term the decisions you make end  up being very different and so things  like culture for example if your culture  you know suddenly goes downhill tomorrow  or assembly improves tomorrow you're not  going to see the effects in this quarter  or this year or even in terms of your  bottom line but two or three years down  the road you will and same thing with  customer service if if we hypothetically  stopped answering our phones and fired  everyone in our call center that would  actually have a huge gain in our profits  for 2009 I don't think it would hurt our  top-line revenue we'd still do over a  billion and growth sales but two or  three years from now then we'd start  feeling the effects so I think the  biggest thing is just how how long-term  are you thinking I always like doing  these thought experiments and so one way  to think that it is if if money were no  issue if you had infinite money what  would you do or the counter to it is if  everything was free or if this one thing  was free then what would you do  differently and so I think that can help  spark the initial ideas and then once  you kind of have the crazy out there  idea then you work backwards and make it  more into reality for example a lot of  startup tech companies have problems  hiring tech talent so what if there's a  line out the door of amazing engineers  then what would you do differently and  then that might shift the thinking into  okay how do we get a line out the door  of really talented engineers and and  make that the problem the real problem  that you'r